1
00:00:07,264 --> 00:00:09,180
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:09,181 --> 00:00:13,740
This time, let's continue coloring the clothing.

3
00:00:13,741 --> 00:00:16,379
It's not much different from coloring the skin.

4
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,700
Let me zoom in a bit and render.

5
00:00:19,985 --> 00:00:24,490
While looking at the whole picture on the left.

6
00:00:24,491 --> 00:00:29,530
Rendering that brings out this kind of thickness makes it look dense.

7
00:00:32,565 --> 00:00:36,711
I paint while adding to or modifying the design.

8
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:41,600
My hobby is model making.

9
00:00:41,601 --> 00:00:45,920
From my late teens to early twenties, I was into paper models.

10
00:00:45,921 --> 00:00:50,320
There was a time when I made paper models while doing 3D modeling, clumsy as it was.

11
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:57,800
Back then, I made a lot of robots, which are easy to model and effective to make in paper.

12
00:00:58,084 --> 00:00:59,680
You know Gundam, right?

13
00:00:59,681 --> 00:01:05,120
Like this picture, I favored designs with a moderate amount of detail, with panels divided up.

14
00:01:05,121 --> 00:01:07,840
Not just me, but other people into models too.

15
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:10,280
You'll relate.

16
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:15,080
These days, I'm so busy drawing that I'm satisfied with plastic models.

17
00:01:15,081 --> 00:01:17,640
Do you know a horse with one horn?

18
00:01:17,641 --> 00:01:18,120
A unicorn.

19
00:01:18,519 --> 00:01:20,520
I'm obsessed with a robot of that name.

20
00:01:21,909 --> 00:01:26,120
When I designed this outfit for the body, I got the inspiration from there.

21
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:30,440
The finely split details on a pure white surface were really beautiful.

22
00:01:32,059 --> 00:01:38,830
Applying inspiration from somewhere, transformed to fit my picture, is really fun.

23
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:42,711
So you should observe a lot in daily life and get inspiration.

24
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:26,460
Giving each panel its own individual curve a bit like this makes it less boring.

25
00:02:27,555 --> 00:02:32,940
The things we think of as flat are actually often curved.

26
00:02:32,941 --> 00:02:36,341
Subtle curves can add a sense of luxury.

27
00:02:37,149 --> 00:02:39,520
Like the curved surface of a PlayStation 5.

28
00:02:40,325 --> 00:02:43,721
And when drawing belts or bags like these.

29
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:51,740
Let me proceed steadily, with the feel of covering over a line drawing.

30
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:58,220
Honestly, by this point, the picture barely changes relative to the time you put in, so it's tiring and hard.

31
00:05:59,740 --> 00:06:04,030
Around this time, I turn on YouTube and work.

32
00:06:04,031 --> 00:06:07,070
These days, I'm doing a one-day-on, one-day-off road trip.

33
00:06:07,071 --> 00:06:10,030
It's nice because I can just glance at the screen.

34
00:06:10,031 --> 00:06:13,511
Foreign language videos that need subtitles would be hard.

35
00:06:13,930 --> 00:06:19,130
Listening to a radio is good too, and eating snacks while working is good too.

36
00:06:19,131 --> 00:06:22,691
It's a boring task, but I hope I can draw it happily.

37
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:25,850
Honestly, it's boring, but what can you do.

38
00:06:25,851 --> 00:06:27,770
If you don't do it, you fall behind.

39
00:06:27,771 --> 00:06:33,091
I'm grateful that I can make a living doing what I at least like.

40
00:06:33,845 --> 00:06:36,190
I think viewers will be bored too.

41
00:06:36,191 --> 00:06:39,991
Honestly, this chapter is one you don't need to watch intently.

42
00:06:41,370 --> 00:06:47,740
You can watch it listening to music, or playing a game with a cup of coffee.

43
00:06:49,719 --> 00:06:51,290
How do you watch this without speeding up?

44
00:06:52,760 --> 00:06:55,210
I'm running out of things to say.

45
00:06:55,211 --> 00:06:59,011
There'll be about five minutes of rendering with no commentary.

46
00:08:56,325 --> 00:09:01,360
Drawing the seam lines of the clothing like this can be an important point.

47
00:09:01,361 --> 00:09:04,841
It's one of the elements that make the clothing look like it really exists.

48
00:09:06,430 --> 00:09:10,240
Same with buckles or buttons.

49
00:09:10,241 --> 00:09:15,440
The more you thoroughly express these things and they accumulate, the more convincing it becomes.

50
00:09:15,441 --> 00:09:18,960
The simpler the design, the more this is a part you have to take care of.

51
00:09:20,495 --> 00:09:25,480
And lines like these seam lines can help with volume.

52
00:09:25,481 --> 00:09:31,921
Compared to an empty plane with nothing, having lines drawn like this makes the form easier to feel visually.

53
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:40,120
When rendering parts with clear boundaries, I sometimes use the lasso tool like this.

54
00:09:40,121 --> 00:09:44,600
Especially when I worked on League of Legends splash art, I used it often.

55
00:09:46,390 --> 00:09:50,840
Splash art has an extremely clean, boundary-oriented style.

56
00:09:50,841 --> 00:09:54,561
Since it was so different from my drawing style, I remember struggling.

57
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:59,550
I like pictures that are moderately untidy.

58
00:09:59,551 --> 00:10:02,510
I think it's because I'm the type to live in a sloppy way.

59
00:10:02,511 --> 00:10:08,910
Where do my style of cleaning sloppily and being sloppy about everything also appear in my pictures?

60
00:10:08,911 --> 00:10:13,070
But I get the feeling that could become an advantage in realistic-style pictures.

61
00:10:16,385 --> 00:10:21,340
I don't have good eyesight, and in the past I used to draw without glasses.

62
00:10:22,750 --> 00:10:26,041
Since it looked blurry, I often just fudged it.

63
00:10:27,235 --> 00:10:36,030
Since I couldn't see, I drew sloppily, but after I started wearing glasses, it's so clear that I couldn't draw sloppily, which made me sad.

64
00:10:36,915 --> 00:10:38,670
My work time doubled.

65
00:10:40,015 --> 00:10:46,641
What's sad is there's not much difference between the fudged picture and the meticulously detailed one.

66
00:10:47,505 --> 00:10:51,370
Viewers don't even pay attention.

67
00:10:51,371 --> 00:10:56,730
Even if the picture looks messy zoomed in, when you shrink it down, there's not much difference.

68
00:10:56,731 --> 00:11:02,730
Rather, the untidy, free-spirited brush touch can even be a strength at times.

69
00:11:02,731 --> 00:11:05,731
So what I want to say is that balance control is important.

70
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:11,070
To the point of being excessive, there's no need to burn physical energy.

71
00:11:11,071 --> 00:11:14,670
I think moderation is good for everything.

72
00:11:14,671 --> 00:11:17,070
Both rendering and design.

73
00:11:18,835 --> 00:11:23,020
But if a client requests a cleanly organized picture, I have no choice.

74
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:31,601
Since I decided to focus attention on the upper body, it's good to reduce contrast as it goes to the lower body.

75
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,640
To reduce the dispersion of attention.

76
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:40,200
In almost all cases, I concentrate attention on the upper body.

77
00:13:40,201 --> 00:13:42,680
That might be because I'm a concept artist.

78
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:48,170
As I explained before, since you make a lot of games with a quarter view.

79
00:13:51,695 --> 00:13:59,720
When doing boring work like this, you really should listen to music or something, but at companies there are people who don't even allow that.

80
00:14:00,440 --> 00:14:02,520
It's just a pity.

81
00:14:02,521 --> 00:14:06,760
There's also the story that allowing music while working boosted productivity.

82
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:17,911
By this point, progress is really slow relative to the time put in.

83
00:14:19,055 --> 00:14:24,040
Like when you're learning something, knowledge piles up rapidly, then at some point it slows down.

84
00:14:25,470 --> 00:14:27,680
It's the same with massively multiplayer online role-playing games.

85
00:14:27,681 --> 00:14:33,481
You can reach a certain high level relatively quickly, but going above that is hard.

86
00:14:34,085 --> 00:14:46,189
But as a week, a month, a year, five years of that difference accumulates, at some point you see a super high-level ranker character whose combat power is noticeably different from even the highest-level users.

87
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:50,700
I think drawing is the same.

88
00:14:50,701 --> 00:14:54,860
And if you keep drawing, the completion can only improve.

89
00:14:54,861 --> 00:15:03,180
If you think with your head, resolve to move forward, and draw, then today's picture will definitely be better than yesterday's picture.

90
00:16:15,085 --> 00:16:18,440
Shall we compare how much it changed?

91
00:16:18,441 --> 00:16:22,001
Compared to the drawing time, it changed really subtly.

92
00:16:22,915 --> 00:16:25,070
That's an unavoidable part.

93
00:16:25,071 --> 00:16:28,711
Let me keep building it up and compare again later.

94
00:16:31,260 --> 00:16:40,951
Comparing, the clothes seemed to have gotten a bit darker, so the inherent value seems slightly broken. Let me correct the white clothing part with a curve adjustment.

95
00:17:08,119 --> 00:17:13,690
Since I merged the layers, the colored parts got adjusted too.

96
00:17:13,691 --> 00:17:17,491
Let me revert to before the curve adjustment with the history brush.

97
00:17:19,984 --> 00:17:24,410
Before the curve adjustment, check the check box and paint.

98
00:17:47,854 --> 00:17:52,200
Comparing with the first-pass coloring, the picture has progressed considerably.

99
00:17:54,599 --> 00:17:58,840
Next time, let's race to the completion stage of the picture.
